<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catalan Atlas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1375</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Catalan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> is a world map drawn on vellum leaves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> around 1375, attributed to the Jewish cartographer Abraham Cresques. Combining the precise coastlines of nautical charts with information from travellers, it shows the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> holding a gold nugget. It entered the French royal library soon after and is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheque-nationale-de-france">Bibliothèque nationale de France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas.jpg" alt="Catalan Atlas" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cresques Abraham · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Compass_rose_from_Catalan_Atlas_(1375).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Catalan Atlas is a medieval world map, probably made in the late 1370s or early 1380s and often conventionally dated 1375. It was produced by Cresques Abraham, a Jewish cartographer and the leader of the Majorcan cartographic school, whom a contemporary described as a master of world maps, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compasses</a> and illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. It was in the royal library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by 1380, in the reign of Charles V. It originally consisted of six vellum leaves, each about 64.5 by 50 centimetres, painted in various colours including gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. They were later mounted on five wooden panels in a leather binding made by Simon Vostre about 1515, restored most recently in 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The first two leaves carry Catalan texts and illustrations on cosmography, astronomy and astrology, stressing the Earth's spherical shape and giving sailors information on tides and on telling the time at night. The other four leaves form the map itself, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> near the centre. Two show the Orient, and two show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. Cities are drawn with a cross if Christian and a dome if not, and a flag marks each one's allegiance. The western part resembles contemporary portolan charts but has the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> rose known on such a chart. The eastern part combines older world maps with travel literature, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>'s Book of Marvels and Mandeville's Travels, and many Indian and Chinese cities can be identified.</p><p>Its captions name rulers across the known world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> appears under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a>, called Musse Melly, who is described as the richest and noblest lord of that region because of the gold taken from his lands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, the Il-Khanate ruler at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> are also shown. In the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> a King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organa">Organa</a> is placed; no medieval African kingdom bore that name, and historians have suggested it stands for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem–Bornu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The atlas has been described as the most important map of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> in the Catalan language and as the zenith of medieval map-work. Its caption on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> is often misquoted in modern media as a claim about the richest man in the world, and Hadrien Collet has examined it as one of the earliest European stages in the long making of the Mansa Musa wealth myth. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is still preserved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheque-nationale-de-france">Bibliothèque nationale de France</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Catalan Atlas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sicily and Sardinia supplied grain and silver, and Majorcan cartographers produced works such as the <b>Catalan Atlas</b> of 1375.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Mansa Musa spent so much gold in Cairo on his 1324 pilgrimage that it was remembered for years, and Mali soon appeared on the <b>Catalan Atlas</b> of 1375.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom was a Mediterranean trading state, with Palma and Perpignan as royal seats, merchant fleets reaching North Africa and a famed school of cartographers who made the <b>Catalan Atlas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a></li></ul></section>
